MHG. ''borg'', G. ''borg'', pledge, security), < ''beorgan'' (pp. ''borgen'') = D. and G. ''bergen'', protect, secure: see ''borough''<sup>1</sup>. The verb ''borrow''<sup>1</sup> is from the noun.] '''1.''' A pledge or surety; bail; security: applied both to the thing given as security and to the person giving it: as, “with baile nor ''borrowe'',” ''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., May.
{{smaller block|Ye may retain as ''borrow'' my two priests. {{right|''Scott.''}}}}
'''2.''' A borrowing; the act of borrowing.
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet of your royal presence I’ll adventure
The ''borrow'' of a week.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., i. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' Cost; expense.
{{smaller block|That great Pan bought with deare ''borrow''.{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., Sept.}}}}
'''4.''' A tithing; a frank-pledge.

'''borrow'''<sup>1</sup> (bor′ō), ''v.'' [< ME. ''borowen'', ''borwen'', etc., < AS. ''borgian'' (= OFries. ''borga'' = D. ''borgen'' (> prob. Icel. ''borga'' = Sw. ''borga'' = Dan. ''borge'') = OHG. ''borgēn'', MHG. G. ''borgen''), borrow, lit. give a pledge, < ''borh'', ''borg'', a pledge, security: see ''borrow''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To take or obtain (a thing) on pledge given for its return, or without pledge, but on the understanding that the thing obtained is to be returned, or an equivalent of the same kind is to be substituted for it; hence, to obtain the temporary use of: with ''of'' or ''from'' (formerly ''at''): as, to ''borrow'' a book ''from'' a friend; to ''borrow'' money ''of'' a stranger.
{{smaller block|We have ''borrowed'' money for the king’s tribute, and that upon our lands and vineyards. {{right|Neh. v. 4.}}}}
'''2.''' To take or receive gratuitously from another or from a foreign source and apply to one’s own

readings.c2s3:
use; adopt; appropriate; by euphemism, to steal or plagiarize: as, to ''borrow'' aid; English has many ''borrowed'' words; to ''borrow'' an author’s style, ideas, or language.
{{smaller block|These verbal signs they sometimes ''borrow'' from others, and sometimes make themselves.
{{right|''Locke.''}}
It is not hard for any man who hath a Bible in his hands to ''borrow'' good words and holy sayings in abundance.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xxv.}}
That is the way we are strong by ''borrowing'' the might of the elements.
{{right|''Emerson'', Civilization.}}}}
'''3.''' To assume or usurp, as something counterfeit, feigned, or not real; assume out of some pretense.
{{smaller block|<poem>Those ''borrow’d'' tears that Sinon sheds.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 1549.}}
<poem>Each part, depriv’d of supple government,
Shall, stiff and stark and cold, appear like death:
And in this ''borrow’d'' likeness of shrunk death
Thou shalt continue two-and-forty hours.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., iv. 1.}}}}
'''4†.''' To be surety for; hence, to redeem; ransom.
{{smaller block|<poem>I pray you, let me ''borrow'' my arms again.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To practise borrowing; take or receive loans; appropriate to one’s self what belongs to another or others: as, I neither ''borrow'' nor lend; he ''borrows'' freely from other authors.
{{smaller block|<poem>Whoever ''borrow’d'' could not be to blame,
Since the whole House did afterwards the same.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Epil. to Satires, ii. 169.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''borrow'''<sup>2</sup> (bor′ō), ''v. i.'' [Origin uncertain; prob. orig. ‘take shelter’; cf. ''burrow''<sup>2</sup>, shelter.] ''Naut.'', to approach either land or the wind closely. ''Smyth.''

'''borrow'''<sup>3</sup>†, ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''borough''<sup>1</sup>.

'''borrower''' (bor′ō-ėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' One who borrows: opposed to ''lender''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Neither a ''borrower'' nor a lender be:
For loan oft loseth both itself and friend;
And borrowing dulls the edge of husbandry.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' One who takes what belongs to another, and uses it as his own; specifically, in literature, a plagiarist.
{{smaller block|Some say I am a great ''borrower.'' {{right|''Pope.''}}}}

'''borrow-head'''†, ''n.'' [Also written ''borough-head''; orig. (AS.) *''frithborhheáfod'', written ''frithborhheved'' in the (Latin) laws of Edward the Confessor; < ''frithborh'', a tithing (< ''frith'', peace, + ''borh'', pledge, security: see ''borrow''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''), + ''heáfod'', head.] The head of a tithing; a headborough or borsholder.

'''borrowing''' (bor′ō-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''borrow''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of taking or obtaining anything on loan or at second-hand.—'''2.''' The act of taking and using as one’s own.
{{smaller block|Such kind of ''borrowing'' as this, if it be not better’d by the Borrower, among good Authors is accounted Plagiarie. {{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xxiii.}}}}
'''3.''' The thing borrowed.
{{smaller block|Yet are not these thefts but ''borrowings''; not impious falsities, but elegant flowers of speech.}}

